WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.540
Every pathway in this course has been running in your body the whole time you read

2
00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:09.060
it, but at different rates.

3
00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:13.920
Many are opposites that could waste energy if both ran flat out in one cell.

4
00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:19.080
So the body turns them up and down in a timed sequence, as you move from fed to

5
00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:20.080
fasting to starving.

6
00:00:21.183 --> 00:00:25.563
Watch the hours after a meal and you see a relay, each runner handing blood glucose

7
00:00:25.563 --> 00:00:30.143
to the next. Right after eating, glucose floods in from the gut and insulin rises.

8
00:00:31.063 --> 00:00:32.843
Liver, muscle, and fat hear: store.

9
00:00:33.323 --> 00:00:36.243
The liver lays down glycogen, and muscle takes up glucose.

10
00:00:36.863 --> 00:00:41.383
From four to sixteen hours, no more dietary glucose arrives, and glucagon rises.

11
00:00:42.043 --> 00:00:45.343
The liver now maintains blood sugar by breaking down its glycogen.

12
00:00:45.843 --> 00:00:48.723
That store is finite, and largely gone within about a day.

13
00:00:49.363 --> 00:00:54.823
By the end of an overnight fast, making new glucose already supplies roughly as much

14
00:00:54.823 --> 00:00:56.243
as glycogen breakdown does.

15
00:00:57.217 --> 00:00:59.697
Next comes fasting, at one to two days.

16
00:01:00.457 --> 00:01:02.717
Gluconeogenesis grows as glycogen dwindles.

17
00:01:03.277 --> 00:01:07.377
The liver, and more and more the kidney, makes glucose from scratch, drawing on

18
00:01:07.377 --> 00:01:10.537
lactate, the amino acid alanine, and glycerol.

19
00:01:11.277 --> 00:01:14.257
Meanwhile adipose tissue releases free fatty acids.

20
00:01:14.777 --> 00:01:17.117
These become the major fuel for muscle and liver.

21
00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:20.890
In starvation, the brain is the problem child.

22
00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:26.730
It normally burns about 120 grams of glucose a day, and long-chain fatty acids are

23
00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:27.770
only a minor fuel.

24
00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:32.970
They reach brain tissue, where astrocytes burn some, but neurons rely on other

25
00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:38.390
fuels. If glucose were the only option, the body would make all 120 grams by

26
00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:42.690
gluconeogenesis. The main raw material would be your own muscle protein.

27
00:01:43.210 --> 00:01:44.730
You would consume yourself within weeks.

28
00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:47.460
Ketone bodies are the escape.

29
00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:51.020
They are small and water-soluble, and they cross into the brain.

30
00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:56.700
In starvation, liver ketone production climbs, as the flood of fatty-acid-derived

31
00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:59.540
acetyl-CoA becomes ketone bodies that are exported.

32
00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:04.940
Over days to weeks the brain adapts to burn them, cutting its glucose requirement to

33
00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:06.420
roughly 40 grams a day.

34
00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:10.720
That protein-sparing effect decides whether you survive a long fast.

35
00:02:11.783 --> 00:02:14.663
The liver makes ketone bodies but cannot use them.

36
00:02:15.223 --> 00:02:19.743
It lacks SCOT, the enzyme that reactivates acetoacetate so it can be burned.

37
00:02:20.383 --> 00:02:23.943
So everything it produces is shipped out to the brain, heart, and muscle.

38
00:02:24.783 --> 00:02:26.623
Ketogenesis is an export business.
